  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B018",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B046 remains the most defensible final configuration because it joins a precise physical idea—the magic-wavelength lattice—with defining high-performance realization, and because its central performance has been independently reproduced. It is a mature measurement technique rather than a promise of later applications: it has already made a durable platform for precision tests and sensing. The proposed two-person credit is comparatively coherent, provided the citation remains limited to optical lattice clocks and does not absorb the wider history of optical-frequency metrology.",
    "case_against": "The apparent clarity of B046 must not become an assumption. We still need archival confirmation that Katori and Ye appropriately delimit the invention and realization, rather than merely representing a broader chain of essential work. The citation should neither claim all optical-clock development nor rely on a future redefinition of the second. B018 is a powerful mature observational alternative, while B004 retains the special significance of opening a new astronomical messenger channel; both, however, leave more difficult restricted attribution questions.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP because its precision map produced durable, repeatedly checked empirical consequences, while recognizing that three-person credit for a space mission requires scrutiny.",
        "response": "I agree that WMAP is an exceptionally mature empirical achievement and have elevated it as my first alternative. Its distinctive contribution can be cited carefully, but the supplied discussion still leaves open whether Bennett, Spergel, and Page alone fairly span mission, instrument, analysis, and interpretation. For B046, the connection between the defining concept, realization, and named pair is presently more bounded."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction established that scalable quantum computation is physically possible, despite the lack of a fully fault-tolerant scalable logical computer.",
        "response": "I accept the foundational force of that argument and would not require a commercial machine. Yet the missing decisive scalable demonstration remains material when compared with lattice clocks, whose defining physical method is already reproducible at extraordinary performance in independent laboratories. It is therefore premature for me to displace B046 with B027 this year."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson supports room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance for its robust physical realization and lasting device consequences, while treating priority and attribution as central vulnerabilities.",
        "response": "That combination of mature experiment and lasting consequence is compelling. But his reservation is decisive in a final allocation: the supplied record does not yet resolve the boundary among early tunnelling work, room-temperature realization, materials advances, and later engineering. B046 has a narrower and more testable credit boundary, though it too requires final archival review."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone best represent the invention and defining realization of optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the final citation can unambiguously distinguish optical lattice clocks from broader optical-frequency-clock development.",
      "Whether the proposed restricted credit for WMAP or IceCube can fairly represent collaboration-scale achievements if B046's attribution proves incomplete."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
